🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗶𝗻 𝗖𝗿𝗮𝘀𝗵𝗟𝗼𝗼𝗽𝗕𝗮𝗰𝗸𝗢𝗳𝗳 — 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗪𝗼𝘂𝗹𝗱 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗗𝗲𝗯𝘂𝗴 𝗜𝘁?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

Your application Pod starts, crashes after a few seconds, and keeps restarting. Kubernetes reports "CrashLoopBackOff". How would you troubleshoot this issue in production?

Here’s my step-by-step approach 👇

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝘂𝘀

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Check the restart count, container state, exit code, termination reason, and Events.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗣𝗿𝗲𝘃𝗶𝗼𝘂𝘀 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗮𝗶𝗻𝗲𝗿 𝗟𝗼𝗴𝘀

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace> --previous

This is one of the most useful commands when a container keeps restarting.

Look for application exceptions, missing configuration, database connection failures, permission errors, or startup problems.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟯: 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗘𝘅𝗶𝘁 𝗖𝗼𝗱𝗲𝘀

kubectl get pod <pod-name> -n <namespace> -o yaml

Inspect "lastState.terminated", including the exit code and reason.

For example, exit code "137" may indicate that the process was killed, commonly due to memory pressure or an OOM kill. Confirm the actual termination reason before deciding.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟰: 𝗩𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗳𝘆 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝗳𝗶𝗴𝘂𝗿𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻

Check environment variables, ConfigMaps, Secrets, mounted files, and application startup commands. A missing variable or incorrect configuration can cause the process to exit repeatedly.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟱: 𝗥𝗲𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗣𝗿𝗼𝗯𝗲𝘀 𝗮𝗻𝗱 𝗥𝗲𝘀𝗼𝘂𝗿𝗰𝗲𝘀

Check whether liveness probes are restarting the container before the application can initialize. Review memory limits and resource-related termination events.

🔹 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟲: 𝗙𝗶𝘅 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗥𝗼𝗼𝘁 𝗖𝗮𝘂𝘀𝗲

Correct the identified issue, then monitor the Pod and verify that it remains Ready without repeated restarts.

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

"CrashLoopBackOff" is a restart-backoff state, not the root cause itself.

Check previous logs → inspect termination details → validate configuration, probes, and resources → fix the actual problem.
